// @run
// @log 7
// @log 7
// @log 7
// @log 7
// GRADUATED from `tests/cases/soundness/xfail-miscompile-nullable-map-value-spelling-twin.vl`
// (silent-class-inventory D124, closed 2026-08-27). The FIRST program below is that pin
// byte-for-byte: seven lines, no function, no union, no generic, no import, no lambda and
// no `??` — two map builds, one annotation and a store.
//
// WHAT IT PINS is the mv table's HEAP dedup across the `| null` NICHE. `nulRefMapValInnerOf`
// states the contract in its own header: a `{[K]: V | null}` value "resolves its struct/map
// identity — and keys its vals ref-list slot — through the single non-null member, so the
// slot SHARES the vals rep (and the heap dedup) with the non-null twin". The MINT honoured
// the first half and the comparator never honoured the second: `repMapValSlotsTwin` keyed
// on `mvValCanonId`, which reads the value's own arena index, and a `TyNullable`'s
// `repCanonId` is by construction not its inner's. Probed at the mint on this program:
//
//   slot=0 name={[string]:i32}      kind=6 canon=2 rl=0 rlwrap=5 rep=0
//   slot=1 name={[string]:i32}|null kind=6 canon=3 rl=0 rlwrap=5 rep=1
//
// — the SAME vals ref-list slot and the SAME wrapper, so the two 7-field map structs are
// byte-identical, and `canon` is the only column that differs. `rep=1` is the refusal.
//
// THE MESSAGE NAMED NULLABILITY AND THE MECHANISM WAS TWO HEAP TYPES — the third specimen
// in a row of which that is true. Disassembled on master, `$11` and `$12` are field-for-field
// equal map structs, one minted for `{[string]: {[string]: i32}}` (`l2`, inferred) and one
// for its `| null` spelling (the annotation's value); the `ref null` in the sentence is the
// vals ARRAY ELEMENT's own type, which every mv slot has. Read the disassembly, never the
// sentence.
//
// THE AXIS IS THE SECOND SPELLING, not the nullability and not the nesting, and the three
// programs after the pin are the controls that say so — each RAN on master, so this file
// reddens if the fix ever starts deciding by nullability or by depth instead:
//   2. the `| null` dropped from the annotation;
//   3. the inner map MONO (`{[string]: i32}`, whose struct is the shared one, so there is
//      no second slot to mint);
//   4. `l2` annotated with the outer's own nullable-valued spelling, so one layout is
//      named once instead of twice.
//
// The merge is INTERLOCKED rather than trusted: `mAssignTypeIndices` fails loudly when two
// claimed twins resolve different vals wrappers, so an over-merge here is a compiler error
// and never a silent alias.

// 1 — the pin, byte-for-byte
const z = Map()
z["z"] = 1
const l2 = Map()
l2["a"] = z
const c: {[string]: {[string]: {[string]: i32} | null}} = Map()
c["k"] = l2
print(7)

// 2 — control: no `| null` on the annotation (ran on master)
const z2 = Map()
z2["z"] = 1
const m2 = Map()
m2["a"] = z2
const c2: {[string]: {[string]: {[string]: i32}}} = Map()
c2["k"] = m2
print(7)

// 3 — control: a MONO inner map (ran on master)
const m3 = Map()
m3["a"] = 1
const c3: {[string]: {[string]: i32} | null} = Map()
c3["k"] = m3
print(7)

// 4 — control: the inner local annotated with the outer's own spelling (ran on master)
const z4 = Map()
z4["z"] = 1
const l4: {[string]: {[string]: i32} | null} = Map()
l4["a"] = z4
const c4: {[string]: {[string]: {[string]: i32} | null}} = Map()
c4["k"] = l4
print(7)
